<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Lucca</h1><p class="st-meta">1815 CE – 1847 CE · capital Lucca</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Tuscan duchy created in 1815 for the former queen of Etruria, Maria Luisa of Bourbon, and absorbed by Tuscany in 1847.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> gave <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-lucca">Lucca</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-luisa-duchess-of-lucca">Maria Luisa of Spain</a>, former regent of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-etruria">Kingdom of Etruria</a>, as compensation for losing <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Parma</a>, which went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-louise-duchess-of-parma">Marie Louise</a>. Maria Luisa took possession in 1817 and ruled until her death in 1824.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-luisa-duchess-of-lucca">Maria Luisa</a> revived court life, built roads and the aqueduct of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-lucca">Lucca</a> and encouraged the arts. Her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-duke-of-parma">Charles Louis</a>, Duke Charles I, ruled from 1824, an extravagant and unpredictable prince who spent much time abroad and left government to ministers. The duchy was small, conservative and dependent on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> settlement, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a> were to return to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Parma</a> when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-louise-duchess-of-parma">Marie Louise</a> died. In October 1847, shortly before her death, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-duke-of-parma">Charles Louis</a> handed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-lucca">Lucca</a> to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany</a> and later became Duke of Parma.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-lucca.jpg" alt="Duchy of Lucca" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: This vector image includes elements that have been taken or adapted from this file · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Third_Flag_of_the_Duchy_of_Lucca.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1815 CE – 1847 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lucca</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Maria Luisa of Spain, Charles Louis of Bourbon-Parma</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lucca</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1831&amp;focus=duchy-of-lucca" aria-label="Duchy of Lucca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4932 624 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Lucca lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5252 880L5252 880M5252 880L5270 883"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5252 880h0M5270 883h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5252 880h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5252 880h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lucca. <a href="/atlas?year=1831&amp;focus=duchy-of-lucca">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
